I tested end-to-end reachability from the IT HOD’s host (it_hod, 192.168.1.2) to multiple CS department hosts (192.168.2.2–192.168.2.6). All ICMP pings succeeded with 0% loss and ~1.2–1.9 ms RTT, confirming working connectivity at the time of testing. On the routing side, router0—the device that joins the IT (192.168.1.0/24) and CS (192.168.2.0/24) subnets—has both interfaces up (eth0=192.168.1.1, eth1=192.168.2.1) and is correctly routing: both 192.168.1.0/24 and 192.168.2.0/24 are installed as connected routes. Host configurations are correct as well: it_hod’s default gateway is 192.168.1.1 and cs_hod’s is 192.168.2.1. This localizes the previously reported loss to “not currently reproducible.” No access lists, policy, or daemon issues were observed on router0 that would explain persistent inter-department isolation. Root cause: No fault is present now; the network path between IT and CS is operating normally. Corrective fix: None required at present. If the symptom recurs, please capture the exact target IP(s) and timestamp; we will correlate with router0 and switch0/switch1 interface counters and ARP tables to check for intermittent link/ARP issues. For the workstation, a quick workaround during a transient event is to flush the ARP cache or re-disable/enable the interface.
